registryEngine() probes a hard-coded list (5.7, 5.6, 5.5, 5.4), so UE 5.8 (released June 2026) and the 5.9 preview (Nov 2026) are never found via the registry. autodetectEngine() then sorts glob matches as full path strings in reverse, so a D:/ or E:/ install beats a newer C:/ one, and UE_5.10 would sort below UE_5.9. Worst case today: a 5.8 project on a machine that also has 5.7 opens in 5.7. Fix: read EngineAssociation from the .uproject and resolve it first (launcher version -> HKLM\SOFTWARE\EpicGames\Unreal Engine\<ver>; {GUID} source build -> HKCU\Software\Epic Games\Unreal Engine\Builds); otherwise enumerate the registry subkeys instead of a fixed list, and pick the highest version numerically across all candidates. Done = unit tests for the version ordering and association parsing; Windows path changes labelled unverified.

